    "summary": "Stellar Standing On The Moon and a soaring Terrapin‑Station second set",
    "blurb": "Highlights include an electrifying Shakedown Street opener, a powerful Terrapin Station into Drums/Space climax, and Jerry’s screaming guitar on Standing On The Moon. The second set builds emotional momentum, capped by a memorable encore of Black Muddy River.",
    "review": "December 6, 1989 found the Dead channeling the post‑quake benefit energy straight into a show that still feels fresh today. After an adventurous Shakedown Street opener – Brent’s keyboards flirting with new textures while Jerry rides a wave of screaming lead – the first set settles into a solid groove. The second set is where the magic truly ignites: Scarlet Begonias segues into Sugar Magnolia, then rolls into a haunting Ship Of Fools and a soaring Terrapin Station that launches into an earthquake‑charged Drums/Space climax. Jerry’s guitar on Standing On The Moon soars above the mix, and the dual‑drum assault from Mickey and Billy feels both thunderous and tender. The night closes with a heartfelt Black Muddy River encore, leaving the audience buzzing long after the final chord.\n\nFor listeners wanting the clearest window into that night, the Miller soundboard capture (gd89-12-06.sbd.miller.14917.sbeok.shnf) delivers an “excellent” mix praised as crystal‑clear and perfectly balanced – it captures every nuance of the band’s performance without dropouts or distortion, making it the definitive version of this emotionally charged benefit concert.",
